WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.420
Chameleons' tongues are fast and long--

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.540
extending twice their body length in a

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.140
fraction of a second.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.160
To find out just
how fast, researchers used high-speed

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.260
cameras to film different species of
chamaleon

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.940
as they a targeted a suspended cricket.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.540
They found that smaller species had
faster tongues.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.000
The one-centimeter long R. spinosus

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.400
can catapult its tongue towards prey

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.580
at over 2,500 meters per second.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.280
Faster than any reptile, bird, or mammal.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.740
This incredible acceleration is powered by

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.640
14 kilowatts per kilogram of muscle-

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.400
-the highest muscle power output of any

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.460
terrestrial vertebrate.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.800
Smaller chamaleons need to eat more food each day to survive

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.320
and so have proportionally larger tongues
for their body size.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:59.020
This allows them to propel their tongues at accelerations of up to 264 g--

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.900
Too fast for even the nimblest cricket to
outrun.

